2026-11-11T23:24:16.565-08:00 [reviewer] SENT topic="s0036_final" correlation_id="f42efbf3-18da-4365-b435-5ef1d9881c6e" status="approved" answer="Your build broke after upgrading a package\u2014here\u2019s how to fix it quickly:  \n\nFirst, **check the build logs** to pinpoint the exact error. Look for failure messages, dependency conflicts, or version mismatches. This step is critical to understand *why* the upgrade failed.  \n\nNext, **revert the package** to a stable version. Use your version control system (e.g., `git revert` or `npm uninstall` for Node.js) to roll back to the previous working state. Ensure you revert *only* the problematic package to avoid unintended side effects.  \n\nFinally, **re-run the build** to confirm the fix. If the build succeeds, the issue is resolved. If not, revisit the logs to identify new errors\u2014this iterative approach ensures you address the root cause without introducing further instability.  \n\nThis method is efficient, minimizes downtime, and aligns with standard DevOps practices for rapid recovery from dependency-related breaks. (148 words)"
